# usermanagement.bug

> Module: User Management / Permissions (integrated in settings) | Category: CORE  
> Last Tested: 02 June 2026

---

### B-PERM-01: Restricted User (ofis) Has ALL Permissions Set to False

| Field | Detail |
|-------|--------|
| **Severity** | P1 — High |
| **Status** | ⬜ **OPEN** |
| **Found** | API testing — `GET /api/tenant/my-permissions` for ofis@ofis.com returns ALL `can_*: false` |
| **Reproduced** | Login as ofis@ofis.com → call `GET /api/tenant/my-permissions` → every module_key has `can_view:false, can_create:false, can_edit:false, can_delete:false` |
| **Bug** | The ofis@ofis.com user was configured with truck_tracking and expense_tracking permissions during the June 1 bug fix session (verified working: "ofis user with 2 module permissions gets 200 on truck_tracking + expense_tracking, 403 on all other 9 modules"). But on June 2, ALL permissions are `false`. This means: 1) The permissions were never seeded/persisted in the QA database, OR 2) The `SavePermissions` handler defaulted all toggles to `false` when saving without changes, OR 3) The QA database was reset and permissions weren't re-applied. |
| **Impact** | All non-TENANT_OWNER users have ZERO module access. The sidebar shows only Dashboard. Users with roles like OFFICE, DRIVER, ACCOUNTANT cannot access ANY module. The entire role-based access control system is effectively broken because permissions default to all-off. |
| **Root Cause** | Likely one of: 1) `SavePermissions` in `usermanagement.go` defaults to `false` for all modules when the modal is opened and saved without explicit toggles. 2) The `user_permissions` table has no rows for ofis@ofis.com in QA because the database was recreated. 3) The `GetPermissions` handler returns default `false` values when no rows exist in `user_permissions`, but `SavePermissions` needs to keep existing permissions intact if the modal is not modified. |
| **Required Fix** | 1) Verify `user_permissions` table has rows for ofis user in QA. 2) If no rows exist: fix `SavePermissions` to only save explicitly changed permissions, not overwrite all to `false`. 3) Add a default permission template: when creating a new user, copy the tenant owner's default permission set instead of empty. 4) Add `qa.sh db-seed` step that ensures permissions for test users. |
| **Files to Change** | `backend/internal/handlers/usermanagement.go`, `qa.sh` |
| **CI Gate to Add** | `api-smoke`: verify ofis user can access truck_tracking (200) and expense_tracking (200) |

### B-PERM-02: PermissionEnforcer Returns 403 After Permissions Wiped

| Field | Detail |
|-------|--------|
| **Severity** | P1 — High |
| **Status** | ⬜ **OPEN** (consequence of B-PERM-01) |
| **Found** | API testing — ofis user gets 403 on ALL tenant endpoints including truck_tracking |
| **Bug** | Because B-PERM-01 sets all permissions to `false`, the `PermissionEnforcer` middleware correctly returns 403 for every module. The enforcement is working correctly, but the permissions data is wrong. |
| **Impact** | ofis@ofis.com (and any non-owner user) cannot access any module despite being configured to have access. |
| **Dependency** | Fixing B-PERM-01 will resolve B-PERM-02 automatically. |
